	spec: '/status shows every provider account and its usage windows as a table like the old Hal\'s: slot i/n with * on the one the current session uses, the account (email or key name, masked as a***@g****.com), and for each window (5h, 7d, and model-specific ones such as 7d_sonnet) a small bar, "N% used" and when it resets (local time, "on 3 Oct" when not today). API-key accounts show "API key" instead of windows. Data comes from task 61\'s usage tracking; if it is older than a minute the command refreshes it first (with a short timeout, keeping the old data on failure and saying so). Terminal and web render the same table from one data shape. Tests: masking; the reset wording today and another day; the * mark; stale data refreshed. Looks like the old Hal\'s /status (artifacts anthropic-usage.ts, openai-usage.ts, old-md.ts; rule restored 28 Sep after the user compared them): it opens with Runtime: host PID, version (git hash) and when the host started with its uptime; then one table per provider under a heading line ("Anthropic subscriptions:", "OpenAI subscriptions:"), columns Slot | Account | that provider\'s own windows only; each window cell is two lines, the bar (block characters with eighth-steps at the end, like the old usageBarMarker) above "95% used (resets 23:39)" or "(resets 07:59 on 3 Oct)" in 24-hour local time; a window with no data shows ?. The account is the masked email plus the plan in parentheses when known (OpenAI plan_type, e.g. (plus); the email from the id token or usage answer), never "account 3" when an email is available. A failed refresh is a short second line in that account\'s cell (e.g. "login expired", "HTTP 401"), never the whole auth error with file path and advice; one advice line under the tables says how to log in again when any login is broken. Command output is markdown in both clients (terminal item-view renders output items with markdownView, like model text), and a markdown table cell may hold <br> for a line break (common/markdown, both clients).',
